<h1 class="text-4xl font-semibold text-slate-900 mb-6 tracking-tight">Reading Food Intolerance Test Results</h1>
<p class="text-lg text-slate-500 mb-4">Got test results and not sure what they mean? Here is a guide to interpreting common food intolerance test reports.</p>
<p class="text-sm text-slate-400 mb-8">By Dr. Emily Carter, Registered Dietitian · Updated 20 Jul 2026</p>
<Image src={resultsImage} alt="Medical report showing food intolerance test results being reviewed" class="w-full h-auto rounded-xl mb-8" width={800} height={450} loading="lazy" format="webp" />
<h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-slate-900 mt-10 mb-3">What Do IgG Test Results Mean?</h2>
<p class="text-slate-600 mb-4">IgG tests typically show antibody levels for each food tested, often categorised as low, moderate, or high reactivity. However, according to the <strong>NHS</strong> and the <strong>British Dietetic Association (BDA)</strong>, IgG antibodies may simply indicate normal exposure to a food, not intolerance. The <strong>EAACI</strong> has stated that IgG and IgG4 testing "has no clinical utility" for food intolerance diagnosis.</p>
<p class="text-slate-600 mb-4">If your IgG test shows "high reactivity" to several foods, this does not mean you are intolerant to all of them. Restricting your diet based solely on IgG results may lead to unnecessary dietary limitations and potential nutritional deficiencies.</p>
<h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-slate-900 mt-10 mb-3">How Do You Read Hydrogen Breath Test Results?</h2>
<p class="text-slate-600 mb-4">A rise in breath hydrogen of <strong>20 ppm or more</strong> above baseline after consuming a test sugar (lactose, fructose, lactulose) is typically considered positive for malabsorption. This threshold is supported by clinical guidelines from the <strong>American Gastroenterological Association</strong>.</p>
<h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-slate-900 mt-6 mb-2">Understanding Your Numbers</h3>
<ul class="list-disc list-inside text-slate-600 space-y-2 mb-4">
  <li><strong>Less than 10 ppm rise:</strong> Negative — malabsorption unlikely</li>
  <li><strong>10–19 ppm rise:</strong> Borderline — discuss with your GP</li>
  <li><strong>20+ ppm rise:</strong> Positive — malabsorption likely</li>
</ul>
<h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-slate-900 mt-10 mb-3">How Do You Interpret Elimination Diet Results?</h2>
<p class="text-slate-600 mb-4">Clear symptom improvement during elimination followed by recurrence on reintroduction of a specific food is the most reliable evidence of intolerance. According to the <strong>BDA</strong>, a symptom that appears within 24–72 hours of reintroduction is a strong indicator. Document everything in a <a href="/tools/food-diary" class="text-brand-600 hover:text-brand-700 underline">food diary</a> for the most reliable results.</p>
<h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-slate-900 mt-6 mb-2">Signs of a Positive Result</h3>
<ul class="list-disc list-inside text-slate-600 space-y-2 mb-4">
  <li>Noticeable symptom improvement within 2–3 weeks of elimination</li>
  <li>Symptoms return within 24–72 hours of reintroducing the specific food</li>
  <li>The pattern is reproducible across multiple reintroduction attempts</li>
</ul>
<h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-slate-900 mt-10 mb-3">What Should You Do Before Getting Tested?</h2>
<p class="text-slate-600 mb-4">Preparation can significantly affect the accuracy of your test results. Before any food intolerance testing, consider the following:</p>
<div class="space-y-3 mb-8">
<div class="bg-white border border-slate-200 rounded-xl p-4"><h3 class="font-semibold text-slate-900 mb-1 text-sm">Do not start a restrictive diet before testing</h3><p class="text-sm text-slate-600">For coeliac disease screening, you must be eating gluten for at least 6 weeks before the blood test and biopsy. Starting a gluten-free diet prematurely can lead to false-negative results. According to the <strong>BDA</strong>, this is one of the most common mistakes patients make.</p></div>
<div class="bg-white border border-slate-200 rounded-xl p-4"><h3 class="font-semibold text-slate-900 mb-1 text-sm">Keep a symptom diary first</h3><p class="text-sm text-slate-600">Record your meals, symptoms, and timing for at least 2 weeks before your GP appointment. This helps your doctor identify patterns and decide which tests are most appropriate. Use our <a href="/tools/food-diary" class="text-brand-600 hover:text-brand-700 underline">Food Diary</a> to track this digitally.</p></div>
<div class="bg-white border border-slate-200 rounded-xl p-4"><h3 class="font-semibold text-slate-900 mb-1 text-sm">Prepare for breath tests properly</h3><p class="text-sm text-slate-600">For hydrogen breath tests, you will typically need to fast overnight and avoid antibiotics, laxatives, and high-fibre foods for 2–4 weeks beforehand. Follow your clinic's specific preparation instructions carefully, as improper preparation can invalidate results.</p></div>
</div>

<h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-slate-900 mt-10 mb-3">How Do You Create an Action Plan From Your Results?</h2>
<p class="text-slate-600 mb-4">Once you have your test results, work with your GP or dietitian to create a structured action plan:</p>
<ul class="list-disc list-inside text-slate-600 space-y-2 mb-6">
  <li><strong>Positive result:</strong> If a specific intolerance is confirmed, plan a targeted elimination of the trigger food and identify suitable nutritional replacements. For example, if lactose intolerance is confirmed, replace dairy calcium with fortified plant milks, leafy greens, and tofu.</li>
  <li><strong>Negative result:</strong> If tests come back negative but symptoms persist, discuss other potential causes with your GP — including coeliac disease, IBS, inflammatory bowel disease, or other food intolerances not covered by the initial tests.</li>
  <li><strong>Inconclusive result:</strong> Borderline results may require repeat testing or a supervised elimination diet to clarify. According to the <strong>BDA</strong>, an elimination diet followed by controlled reintroduction remains the most reliable way to confirm or rule out a food intolerance.</li>
</ul>

<h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-slate-900 mt-10 mb-3">What Are the Limitations of Food Intolerance Tests?</h2>
<p class="text-slate-600 mb-4">No single test can identify all food intolerances. Key limitations include:</p>
<ul class="list-disc list-inside text-slate-600 space-y-2 mb-6">
  <li><strong>Breath tests</strong> only detect specific sugar malabsorption (lactose, fructose, lactulose) and cannot identify intolerances to food additives, histamine, or salicylates.</li>
  <li><strong>IgG blood tests</strong> are not recommended for intolerance diagnosis by any major medical organisation. They measure exposure, not intolerance, and often lead to unnecessary dietary restrictions.</li>
  <li><strong>Elimination diets</strong> are the most comprehensive approach but require discipline, time (4–8 weeks), and ideally professional supervision to ensure nutritional adequacy.</li>
  <li><strong>Some intolerances</strong> — such as reactions to food additives, salicylates, or amines — have no validated laboratory test and can only be identified through careful elimination and reintroduction under dietitian guidance.</li>
</ul>

<h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-slate-900 mt-10 mb-3">When Should You See a Doctor About Your Results?</h2>
<p class="text-slate-600 mb-4">Always discuss test results with your GP or a registered dietitian before making major dietary changes. Never self-diagnose based on commercial IgG test results alone.</p>

